<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>termesh — a terminal-native, agent-first IDE</title>
<meta name="description" content="A terminal-native IDE where the AI coding agent shares your buffers, diagnostics, git diff and terminal output, and proposes edits as reviewable inline diffs.">
<meta property="og:title" content="termesh">
<meta property="og:description" content="A terminal-native, agent-first IDE. Agent edits arrive as reviewable inline diffs, not a chat box bolted to the side.">
<meta property="og:type" content="website">
<meta property="og:url" content="https://iseif.github.io/termesh/">
<style>
  /* Light is the base; dark redefines only the tokens, so no colour is defined
     exclusively inside a media query. */
  :root {
    --bg: #fbfaf8;
    --panel: #ffffff;
    --ink: #1a1a1a;
    --muted: #5c5a55;
    --rule: #e3e0da;
    --accent: #b3005e;
    --term-bg: #262335;
    --term-ink: #e5e1f0;
    --term-accent: #ff79c6;
  }
  @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
    :root:not([data-theme="light"]) {
      --bg: #17161c;
      --panel: #1e1d25;
      --ink: #ece9f2;
      --muted: #a09cae;
      --rule: #2f2d3a;
      --accent: #ff79c6;
      --term-bg: #100f16;
      --term-ink: #e5e1f0;
      --term-accent: #ff79c6;
    }
  }
  :root[data-theme="dark"] {
    --bg: #17161c;
    --panel: #1e1d25;
    --ink: #ece9f2;
    --muted: #a09cae;
    --rule: #2f2d3a;
    --accent: #ff79c6;
    --term-bg: #100f16;
    --term-ink: #e5e1f0;
    --term-accent: #ff79c6;
  }

  * { box-sizing: border-box; }
  body {
    margin: 0;
    background: var(--bg);
    color: var(--ink);
    font: 16px/1.65 ui-sans-serif, -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, "Segoe UI", Roboto, sans-serif;
    -webkit-font-smoothing: antialiased;
  }
  .wrap { max-width: 62rem; margin: 0 auto; padding: 0 1.25rem; }
  a { color: var(--accent); }

  header { padding: 4.5rem 0 2.5rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  h1 {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: clamp(2.6rem, 7vw, 4rem);
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
    font-weight: 680;
  }
  .tagline {
    margin: 0.85rem 0 0;
    font-size: clamp(1.1rem, 2.6vw, 1.45rem);
    color: var(--muted);
    max-width: 40rem;
  }
  .tagline strong { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 600; }

  .cta { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.7rem; margin-top: 2rem; align-items: center; }
  .btn {
    display: inline-block;
    padding: 0.6rem 1.1rem;
    border-radius: 7px;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    background: var(--panel);
    color: var(--ink);
    text-decoration: none;
    font-weight: 550;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }
  .btn.primary { background: var(--accent); border-color: var(--accent); color: #fff; }
  .install {
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, Consolas, monospace;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    padding: 0.6rem 0.9rem;
    border-radius: 7px;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    background: var(--panel);
    color: var(--muted);
    white-space: nowrap;
    overflow-x: auto;
    max-width: 100%;
  }
  .install b { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 600; }

  section { padding: 3.25rem 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  h2 {
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
    margin: 0 0 0.4rem;
  }
  .lede { color: var(--muted); margin: 0 0 1.75rem; max-width: 44rem; }

  .points { display: grid; gap: 1.4rem; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(15rem, 1fr)); }
  .point h3 { margin: 0 0 0.35rem; font-size: 1rem; }
  .point p { margin: 0; color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.95rem; }

  figure { margin: 0 0 2rem; }
  figcaption {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.6rem;
  }
  figcaption code {
    background: var(--panel);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: 4px;
    padding: 0.08rem 0.35rem;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
  }
  /* The frames are 96 columns of box-drawing characters. They must never wrap, so
     they scroll inside their own box rather than widening the page. */
  .frame {
    background: var(--term-bg);
    color: var(--term-ink);
    border-radius: 9px;
    padding: 1rem 1.1rem;
    overflow-x: auto;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .frame pre {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, Consolas, monospace;
    font-size: 11px;
    line-height: 1.35;
    white-space: pre;
  }
  @media (min-width: 60rem) { .frame pre { font-size: 12.5px; } }

  ul.plain { padding-left: 1.1rem; color: var(--muted); }
  ul.plain li { margin-bottom: 0.5rem; }
  ul.plain strong { color: var(--ink); }

  pre.code {
    background: var(--panel);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 0.9rem 1rem;
    overflow-x: auto;
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, Consolas, monospace;
    font-size: 0.86rem;
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
  }

  footer { padding: 2.5rem 0 4rem; color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.9rem; }
  footer a { color: var(--muted); }
</style>
</head>
<body>

<header>
  <div class="wrap">
    <h1>termesh</h1>
    <p class="tagline">
      A terminal-native, <strong>agent-first</strong> IDE. The AI coding agent shares your open
      buffers, diagnostics, git diff and terminal output — and proposes edits as
      <strong>reviewable inline diffs</strong>, not a chat box bolted to the side.
    </p>
    <div class="cta">
      <a class="btn primary" href="https://github.com/iseif/termesh">GitHub</a>
      <a class="btn" href="https://github.com/iseif/termesh/releases/latest">Download {{VERSION}}</a>
      <a class="btn" href="https://crates.io/crates/termesh">crates.io</a>
      <span class="install"><b>cargo install termesh --locked</b></span>
    </div>
  </div>
</header>

<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>Why this exists</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      The terminal already has excellent focused tools. Rebuilding those adds nothing. The part
      nobody has built well in a terminal is the agent being a structural peer rather than a
      sidebar.
    </p>
    <div class="points">
      <div class="point">
        <h3>The agent edits through the same spine you do</h3>
        <p>Human and agent drive one action registry and one transaction-based edit path. Proposals
        arrive as diffs in the editor — accept, reject, or accept per hunk. Nothing reaches disk
        until you accept, and a hunk you edited inside is flagged rather than overwritten.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="point">
        <h3>Agent-agnostic, by protocol</h3>
        <p>An <a href="https://agentclientprotocol.com">ACP</a> client, so Claude Code, Codex,
        Gemini CLI, OpenCode or Goose all plug in. Bring your own agent and model; no vendor is
        assumed or bundled.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="point">
        <h3>It follows you onto a bare SSH box</h3>
        <p>GUI agent IDEs cannot. This runs anywhere a terminal runs — SSH, containers, minimal
        servers — and degrades cleanly to 16 colours or none.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>What it actually looks like</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      Every frame below is real program output, rendered while this page was built — not a
      screenshot and not a drawing. Each one is reproducible on your machine with no terminal, no
      language server and no agent involved.
    </p>

    <figure>
      <figcaption>An agent proposal waiting for review — the gutter marks the changed line, the
      pane offers accept and reject. <code>termesh --dump-frame . --open README.md --agent-demo</code></figcaption>
      <div class="frame"><pre>{{FRAME_AGENT}}</pre></div>
    </figure>

    <figure>
      <figcaption>A diagnostic in the gutter, a hover explaining it, and the error count in the
      status bar. <code>termesh --dump-frame . --lsp-demo</code></figcaption>
      <div class="frame"><pre>{{FRAME_LSP}}</pre></div>
    </figure>

    <figure>
      <figcaption>Git status as conflicts, staged and worktree groups, with an explicit-index
      commit behind <code>c</code>. <code>termesh --dump-frame . --git-demo</code></figcaption>
      <div class="frame"><pre>{{FRAME_GIT}}</pre></div>
    </figure>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>Integration is tiered, so the bet is de-risked</h2>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li><strong>Tier 0 — any AI CLI, free.</strong> Run it inside a managed terminal pane. No
      agent-specific code at all; it works because the terminal works.</li>
      <li><strong>Tier 1 — native ACP.</strong> Shared project context, inline diff review of
      proposed edits, and permission-gated command execution that shows you the exact program,
      argv, cwd and environment before anything runs.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="lede">Both ship today. The tiering exists so the product stays useful where the hard
    one cannot reach.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>Install</h2>
    <pre class="code">cargo install termesh --locked</pre>
    <p class="lede">
      Or take a signed binary from the
      <a href="https://github.com/iseif/termesh/releases/latest">latest release</a> — Linux
      (x86-64, AArch64), macOS (Intel, Apple Silicon) and Windows (x86-64), each with
      <code>SHA256SUMS</code>. The macOS builds are signed with a Developer ID certificate and
      notarized, so a browser download runs without a Gatekeeper prompt. Requires Rust 1.88 or
      newer to build from source.
    </p>
    <pre class="code">termesh .                    # open a project
termesh                      # reopen the last one
termesh --dump-frame . --lsp-demo    # see a frame without a TTY</pre>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>This is a public beta, and the edges are written down</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      The <a href="https://github.com/iseif/termesh/blob/main/docs/support.md">support
      boundaries</a> say exactly what is tested and what is not, rather than leaving you to find
      out. The ones most likely to matter:
    </p>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li>Windows is <strong>best-effort, not Tier 1</strong> — everything passes there except
      pseudo-terminal teardown, which is unverified.</li>
      <li>Language-server support covers Rust, TypeScript/JavaScript, Python and Java. <strong>Syntax
      highlighting is a separate thing</strong> and ships one grammar, Rust — the others get full
      language intelligence but render as plain text.</li>
      <li>An agent session does not survive a restart. Transcript history returns read-only and a
      fresh session starts, because the implemented ACP baseline has no usable session-load path.</li>
      <li>No debugger, plugin system, or multi-agent orchestration yet.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<footer>
  <div class="wrap">
    <p>
      Dual-licensed <a href="https://github.com/iseif/termesh/blob/main/LICENSE-MIT">MIT</a> or
      <a href="https://github.com/iseif/termesh/blob/main/LICENSE-APACHE">Apache-2.0</a>.
      &nbsp;·&nbsp;
